"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 8.4 and an ISUP grade group 2 Gleason pattern on the prostatectomy specimen. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion. Countervailing points are the absence of nodal sampling, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. The biopsy history shows a progression from Gleason 0+0 to Gleason 3+4, suggesting potential underlying aggressiveness not fully captured by the initial assessment.\n\nThe prognosis would be significantly revised with postoperative PSA measurements and their subsequent trajectory. A rising or persistently elevated PSA warrants re-evaluation of risk stratification. Given the lack of nodal sampling, this remains a key source of uncertainty. The current data are insufficient to provide a definitive long-term outlook without further biochemical monitoring. The fixed estimated horizon is 76.69 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 49.85–103.54 months; its coverage has not been validated."
